    "## LESSON 7: Removing Noise From Short-Read Raw Data: Trimming and Filtering Workflows\n",
    "\n",
    "We've reviewed our quality control (QC) reports and taken an \"X-ray\" of our raw data. Now we need to remove sequencing-induced signal noise to prepare our raw data for the alignment (mapping) step with tools like BWA-MEM.\n",
    "\n",
    "Removing noise from raw data in bioinformatics consists of two distinct, sequential mechanical steps:\n",
    "1. **Trimming:** Cutting off technical adapter sequences or bases with dropped Phred quality at the ends of a read.\n",
    "2. **Filtering:** After trimming is complete, asking \"Is what's left of this read still long/good enough for my analysis?\" and removing reads that don't meet the criteria from the dataset entirely.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7.1. Short-Read Raw Data: Illumina and Ion Torrent Approaches\n",
    "* **Illumina (Paired-End) Data:** Offers a high-throughput raw data profile, but with increasing substitution noise toward the ends of reads. To avoid breaking the synchronization between the forward (R1) and reverse (R2) read files, noise removal must be run simultaneously (in paired-end mode).\n",
    "* **Ion Torrent (Single-End) Data:** Usually read in a single direction. Substitution errors are rare, but the raw data has a lot of deletion/insertion noise in homopolymer regions. So trimming and filtering are carried out on a single file.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7.2. Tool We'll Use: Cutadapt (All-in-One Solution)\n",
    "We're installing `Cutadapt`, the industry-standard tool that can do both trimming and filtering on short-read raw data at the same time, with a lot of parameter flexibility, on our virtual machine."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# LESSON 7.2. COLAB TERMINAL GUIDE: Installing Cutadapt\n",
    "print(\"Installing the short-read noise removal tool (Cutadapt)...\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "!sudo apt-get update\n",
    "!sudo apt-get install cutadapt -y\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nGreat! Cutadapt installation completed successfully.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 7.3. Applying It: Trimming and Filtering the Illumina Raw Data\n",
    "The Cutadapt command in the cell below performs the two tasks we've been reviewing on our raw data:\n",
    "* `-a` and `-A` **(Trimming):** cuts the universal Illumina adapter noise from the forward and reverse reads.\n",
    "* `-q 20` **(Trimming):** shaves off noisy end regions where the Phred quality score drops below 20.\n",
    "* `-m 30` **(Filtering):** completely filters out reads from the dataset that end up shorter than 30 bases after trimming, since they're no longer useful."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# LESSON 7.3. COLAB APPLICATION: Illumina Workflow With Cutadapt\n",
    "!mkdir -p filtered_reads\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"TRIMMING AND FILTERING the ILLUMINA (Paired-End) raw data...\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "!cutadapt -a AGATCGGAAGAG -A AGATCGGAAGAG \\\n",
    "    -q 20 -m 30 \\\n",
    "    -o filtered_reads/filtered_SRR13680736_1.fastq \\\n",
    "    -p filtered_reads/filtered_SRR13680736_2.fastq \\\n",
    "    SRR13680736_1.fastq SRR13680736_2.fastq\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nNoise removal for the Illumina data completed successfully. Clean outputs are in the 'filtered_reads/' folder.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 7.4. Footnote: What If We Work With Ion Torrent Raw Data in the Future?\n",
    "\n",
    "Our current project is based on hybrid Illumina and Nanopore raw data. But if one day in your career you come across raw data from an Ion Torrent instrument, your strategy and command structure needs to change as follows:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Single-File Handling:** files will be in single-direction (**Single-End**) format, not a pair (R1/R2).\n",
    "2. **Adapter Change:** instead of the universal Illumina adapters, you need to trim the instrument-specific Ion A or P1 technical adapter sequences.\n",
    "3. **Command Structure:** since there's no reverse read file, the `-A` (second adapter) and `-p` (second output file) parameters are not used in the `Cutadapt` command. You only proceed with the `-a`, `-q` and `-m` parameters.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Application Tip:** If you later download an independent accession produced with Ion Torrent technology (mostly Single-End FASTQ) from the NCBI SRA or ENA databases, you can experience the noise removal and filtering steps yourself by editing the code cell below to match your own file names."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
