    "## LESSON 8: Removing Noise From Long-Read Raw Data: Trimming and Filtering Workflows\n",
    "\n",
    "Even though the N50 value (read length) is very high in raw data obtained from third-generation sequencing technologies, the noise distribution and adapter architecture are platform-specific. So we can't apply the same procedure to Oxford Nanopore and PacBio data; we need to split our strategy in two based on the nature of the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 8.1. Long-Read Raw Data: Oxford Nanopore and PacBio Approaches\n",
    "* **Oxford Nanopore (ONT) Data:** we have to specifically **trim** the motor protein residues left at the start of reads from the raw data. We then need to **filter** this trimmed dataset by quality and length. *(In short, ONT workflow: Trimming + Filtering).*\n",
    "* **PacBio (SMRT/HiFi) Data:** on modern instruments, circular adapters (SMRTbell) are usually trimmed in hardware by the instrument's internal software and never make it into the raw data at all. So since there's no adapter left to trim, this step is skipped; only **filtering** is applied, to remove very short reads that could disturb the signal in variant analysis. *(In short, PacBio workflow: Filtering only).*\n",
    "\n",
    "### 8.2. Pedagogical Split of the Tools We'll Use (Porechop vs. Filtlong)\n",
    "We split our tools' job descriptions based on the nature of the platforms as follows:\n",
    "1. **`Porechop` (ONT Only - Trimming Tool):** a specific tool designed only to detect and cut adapters unique to Oxford Nanopore data. **NEVER USED** on PacBio raw data.\n",
    "2. **`Filtlong` (Universal Long-Read Filter):** a general quality/length filter usable for both technologies (ONT and PacBio). It acts as an aggressive quality filter on ONT data; on PacBio data, whose raw accuracy is already very high (>99.9%), it's used with looser parameters just to remove very short reads."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# LESSON 8.2. COLAB TERMINAL GUIDE: Installing the Long-Read Tools\n",
    "print(\"Installing the long-read noise removal tools (Porechop and Filtlong)...\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "!sudo apt-get install porechop filtlong -y\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nInstallation completed successfully. We can move on to the application now.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 8.3. Applying It: Trimming and Filtering Workflow for Oxford Nanopore (ONT) Raw Data\n",
    "\n",
    "We run the noise removal process on the long-read raw data we obtained from the Oxford Nanopore platform in two steps:\n",
    "1. **Porechop (Trimming):** trims technical motor protein adapter sequences that may remain at the start and end of reads.\n",
    "2. **Filtlong (Filtering):** evaluates the trimmed reads as a whole and removes low-quality and very short reads from the dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "**ONT Filtering Parameter Logic:**\n",
    "* `--min_length 1000`: removes reads shorter than 1000 bases so they don't create noise in signal analysis and de novo genome assembly steps.\n",
    "* `--keep_percent 90`: keeps the highest-quality 90% of the dataset, completely filtering out the worst 10%, which has a high raw error rate."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# LESSON 8.3. COLAB APPLICATION: Oxford Nanopore (ONT) Pipeline Execution\n",
    "!mkdir -p filtered_reads\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"--- PART 1: OXFORD NANOPORE (ONT) WORKFLOW ---\")\n",
    "print(\"Step 1/2: TRIMMING the motor protein adapters in the ONT data with Porechop...\\n\")\n",
    "!porechop -i SRR13680735.fastq -o filtered_reads/trimmed_ONT.fastq\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nStep 2/2: FILTERING the trimmed ONT reads with Filtlong...\\n\")\n",
    "!filtlong --min_length 1000 --keep_percent 90 filtered_reads/trimmed_ONT.fastq > filtered_reads/filtered_ONT.fastq\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nTrimming and filtering steps for the Oxford Nanopore raw data completed successfully!\")"
   ]
